<Panel heading="Expiring documents">

Using **document expiration**, you can schedule RavenDB to delete a document at a set time.  
This way, data that is needed only for a limited time is removed once the set time has passed, with no further action
required from your application.  

For example:

* A shopping cart that is kept only for a certain time.
* An email link that expires after a few hours.
* The details of a login session.
* Cached data, like data copied from an SQL server.

---

### The expiration process

1. **Enabling document expiration for a database:**  
   *Document expiration* is **disabled** by default. 
   See [Enabling document expiration](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#enabling-document-expiration) below.

2. **Scheduling a document's expiration:**  
   Add an `@expires` property to the
   [metadata](../../../client-api/session/how-to/get-and-modify-entity-metadata.mdx) of the document you want to
   delete, and set the property's value to the intended expiration time.  
   e.g., `"@expires": "2026-12-01T08:00:00.0000000Z"`  
   See [Scheduling a document's expiration](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#scheduling-a-document-s-expiration) below.

   <Admonition type="note" title="">
   Some RavenDB features may also add the `@expires` property to documents they create.  
   Like any other document scheduled for expiration, a document scheduled by one of these features is deleted only if
   *document expiration* is enabled for the database.

   * AI agents add the `@expires` property to
     the conversation documents they store, if an expiration period is set for the conversation.  
     AI agents also add the `@expires` property to the conversation history documents they store, if an expiration period
     is set for history documents in the agent's configuration.
   * An [embeddings generation task](../../../ai-integration/generating-embeddings/embedding-collections.mdx#expiration-policy)
     always adds the `@expires` property to the documents of its embeddings cache collection, according to the task's
     cache expiration period (90 days by default).
   </Admonition>

3. **Deleting the scheduled document:**  
   The *document expiration* feature periodically checks the database for documents whose expiration time has passed (every
   60 seconds by default).

   * At the first check after a document's expiration time, the document is deleted.
   * The document may be deleted in a later check if the number of documents due for deletion exceeds the maximum set for
     a single run using the Studio option
     [Set max number of documents to process in a single run](../../../documents/document-expiration/document-expiration-view.mdx#set-max-number-of-documents-to-process-in-a-single-run).
   * Like any other deleted document, a document deleted by the *document expiration* feature is replaced by a
     [tombstone](../../../monitoring/tombstones/overview.mdx), and its attachments, counters, and time series are deleted with it.

4. **Processing the deletion:**  
   Features that react to the deletion of documents process the deletion.  
   For example:

   * Indexes remove the index entries that refer to the deleted document.
   * The deletion is replicated to the other nodes of the
     [database group](../../../server/clustering/distribution/distributed-database.mdx),
     and to the destinations of [external replication](../../../server/ongoing-tasks/external-replication.mdx) tasks.
   * [ETL tasks](../../../server/ongoing-tasks/etl/basics.mdx) apply the deletion at their destinations.
   * If [revisions](../../../document-extensions/revisions/overview.mdx) are enabled for the document's collection, the
     document's revisions are moved to the [Revisions Bin](../../../studio/database/document-extensions/revisions/revisions-bin.mdx),
     or deleted if the revisions configuration is set to purge the revisions of deleted documents.

</Panel>

<Panel heading="Enabling document expiration">

To enable *document expiration* for a database, send the server a
[ExpirationConfiguration](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#expirationconfiguration) object with its `disabled` property
set to `false`, using the
[ConfigureExpirationOperation](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#configureexpirationoperation) operation.

e.g., the following code enables *document expiration* and sets the interval between checks to five minutes.

```php
// Define the expiration configuration
$expirationConfiguration = new ExpirationConfiguration();

// Enable document expiration for the database
$expirationConfiguration->setDisabled(false);

// Check the database every 5 minutes for documents whose expiration time has passed
$expirationConfiguration->setDeleteFrequencyInSec(300);

// Send the configuration to the server
$store->maintenance()->send(new ConfigureExpirationOperation($expirationConfiguration));
```
<br />

<Admonition type="note" title="">
On a **secure server**, enabling or configuring the *document expiration* feature requires, like other changes to a database's
configuration, a client certificate with `Admin` access to the database (or with an `Operator` or `Cluster Admin`
[security clearance](../../../server/security/authorization/security-clearance-and-permissions.mdx)).  
Setting a document's `@expires` metadata property, on the other hand, is a change to the document, which `Read/Write` access
permits.  
See [Setting up Authentication and Authorization](../../../client-api/setting-up-authentication-and-authorization.mdx)
</Admonition>

The `ExpirationConfiguration` object holds the following settings:

* #### `disabled`
  Set this property to `false` to enable *document expiration*, or to `true` to disable the feature.  
  The default value for this property is `false`, so sending a new `ExpirationConfiguration` object enables *document expiration*
  unless you explicitly set `disabled` to `true`.

* #### `deleteFrequencyInSec`
  Sets the interval, in seconds, between the *document expiration* feature's checks for documents whose expiration time has passed.  
  The default value for this property is 60 seconds.
  - **The `deleteFrequencyInSec` value must be greater than 0:**  
    Sending a value of 0 or less fails with a `RavenException`.  
    e.g., the following code sends a `deleteFrequencyInSec` value of 0 and catches the resulting exception.

    ```php
    try {
        $configuration = new ExpirationConfiguration();
        $configuration->setDisabled(false);
        $configuration->setDeleteFrequencyInSec(0);

        $store->maintenance()->send(new ConfigureExpirationOperation($configuration));
    } catch (RavenException $e) {
        // The exception's message includes:
        // "Expiration delete frequency for database '<database name>' must be greater than 0."
    }
    ```
  - **On a [Community license](https://ravendb.net/buy), the `deleteFrequencyInSec` value must be at least 129600 (36 hours):**  
    Enabling *document expiration* with a lower value, or without setting `deleteFrequencyInSec` (so that the 60-second default
    applies), fails with a `RavenException`.  
    e.g., the code at the beginning of this section, which sets `deleteFrequencyInSec` to 300, fails on a Community license.

</Panel>

<Panel heading="Scheduling a document's expiration">

To schedule a document's expiration, set the `@expires` metadata property of the document to the intended expiration time, and
save the document.

<Admonition type="warning" title="">
The `@expires` metadata property is reserved for the *document expiration* feature.  
Do not use it for any other purpose: RavenDB reads any value set for this property as an expiration time.  
A valid date and time schedules the document's deletion, and any other value makes saving the document fail.
</Admonition>

e.g., the following code stores a new shopping cart document and schedules the document's expiration for one hour from now.

```php
$session = $store->openSession();
try {
    // Store a new document
    $cart = new ShoppingCart();
    $cart->setCustomerId("customers/1-A");
    $session->store($cart, "shoppingCarts/1-A");

    // Set the document's expiration time to one hour from now, in UTC
    $expirationTime = new DateTime("+1 hour", new DateTimeZone("UTC"));
    $session->advanced()->getMetadataFor($cart)->put("@expires", $expirationTime);

    // Save the document
    $session->saveChanges();
} finally {
    $session->close();
}
```
<br />

* #### Setting the expiration time in UTC
  Set the `@expires` property to a `DateTime` object in the UTC time zone (e.g., `new DateTime("+1 hour", new DateTimeZone("UTC"))`).  
  The client sends the object's time with a `Z` suffix, which marks the time as UTC, so the server reads the intended time
  correctly.  
  e.g., `"@expires": "2026-12-01T08:00:00.0000000Z"`

  <Admonition type="note" title="">
  Do not set the property to a `DateTime` object in another time zone (e.g., `new DateTime("+1 hour")`, which uses PHP's default
  time zone).  
  If you set such an object, the client will send the object's time as it is in the object's time zone, with a `Z` suffix,
  and the server will read the time as UTC. Unless the object's time zone is UTC, the document will be deleted at a
  different time than intended.
  </Admonition>

* #### Setting an invalid expiration time
  If the value of the `@expires` property is not a valid date and time, `saveChanges` throws a `RavenException`, and none
  of the session's changes are saved.

* #### Scheduling while *document expiration* is disabled
  Setting the `@expires` property while *document expiration* is disabled for the database still schedules the document's
  expiration:
  - If you enable the feature before the expiration time, the document will be deleted at the feature's first check after
    the expiration time.
  - If the expiration time passes while the feature is disabled, the document will be deleted at the feature's first check
    after you enable the feature.
  - In both cases, the document may be deleted in a later check if the number of documents due for deletion exceeds the
    maximum set for a single run using the Studio option
    [Set max number of documents to process in a single run](../../../documents/document-expiration/document-expiration-view.mdx#set-max-number-of-documents-to-process-in-a-single-run).

* #### Changing or canceling a scheduled expiration
  To change a document's expiration time, set its `@expires` property to a new time and save the document.  
  To cancel the expiration, remove the `@expires` property from the document's metadata using a
  [patch](../../../documents/patching-documents/patch-a-single-document/api-overview.mdx).  
  e.g., `$store->operations()->send(new PatchOperation("shoppingCarts/1-A", null, PatchRequest::forScript("delete this['@metadata']['@expires'];")));`

</Panel>

<Panel heading="Expiration considerations">

* #### Deletion delays
  A document is deleted at the *document expiration* feature's first check after the document's expiration time, not at the
  exact moment the expiration time passes.
  - The deletion may be delayed by up to the interval between checks (set by
    [deleteFrequencyInSec](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#deletefrequencyinsec), 60 seconds by default).
  - The deletion may be delayed further if the number of documents due for deletion exceeds the maximum set for a single run
    using the Studio option
    [Set max number of documents to process in a single run](../../../documents/document-expiration/document-expiration-view.mdx#set-max-number-of-documents-to-process-in-a-single-run).

* #### Documents remain available until they are deleted
  A document whose expiration time has passed is not hidden from your application.  
  Until the document is deleted, loading or querying can still return the document, and indexes still hold the
  document's entries.

* #### Conflicted documents
  A document that is in [conflict](../../../server/clustering/replication/replication-conflicts.mdx) is deleted only if all
  its conflicting versions have an `@expires` property, and the expiration time of all these versions has passed.
  - A document remains in conflict only if the conflict is not resolved automatically: by default, RavenDB resolves
    conflicts to the most recently modified version of the document (see
    [Conflict Detection](../../../server/clustering/replication/replication-conflicts.mdx#conflict-detection)).

* #### Reverting documents to revisions
  When you revert a document to a revision
  (or [revert documents to a specific time](../../../document-extensions/revisions/revert-documents-to-revisions/revert-documents-to-specific-time.mdx)),
  the reverted document gets the content of the revision **without** the revision's `@expires` property, even if the revision
  holds this property.  
  - The `@expires` property is omitted from the reverted document because the revision's expiration time was set when
    the revision was created, and has usually already passed by the time of the revert.  
    Keeping the property would delete the reverted document right after the revert.
  - Since the `@expires` property is omitted, the reverted document will no longer be scheduled for deletion unless you set
    its `@expires` property again.

* #### Exporting and importing documents
  By default, [exporting a database](../../../studio/database/tasks/export-database.mdx) and
  [importing data](../../../studio/database/tasks/import-data/import-data-file.mdx) using Studio include documents whose
  expiration time has passed.  
  To leave these documents out, turn off the **Include Expired Documents** option in Studio's export or import view.  
  Imported documents keep their `@expires` property: if *document expiration* is enabled for the destination database,
  imported documents whose expiration time has passed will be deleted at the feature's first check after the import.

* #### Compare-exchange items
  Compare-exchange items can also be scheduled for expiration by an `@expires` property in their metadata.  
  Their expiration is handled separately, not by the *document expiration* feature.

</Panel>

<Panel heading="Syntax">

<ContentFrame>

### ConfigureExpirationOperation

Sends a `ExpirationConfiguration` object to the server, to enable or disable *document expiration* for a database and configure the
feature.

```php
public function __construct(?ExpirationConfiguration $configuration)
```
<br />
Usage:

```php
$result = $store->maintenance()->send(new ConfigureExpirationOperation($expirationConfiguration));
```
<br />

| Parameter | Type | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **configuration** | [ExpirationConfiguration](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#expirationconfiguration) | The *document expiration* configuration to apply to the database. |

| Return value | |
|-----------|-------------|
| [ConfigureExpirationOperationResult](../../../documents/document-expiration/client-api.mdx#configureexpirationoperationresult) | Contains `raftCommandIndex`. |

</ContentFrame>

